# Day 06: Vintage 🗺️

Part of the [#30DayMapChallenge](https://30daymapchallenge.com/)

For Day 06, I explored historical topographic maps and experimented with bringing historical cartographic data into a modern GIS environment. The project focuses on two locations: **New York City, USA**, and **Nukus, Uzbekistan**.

## 🗽 1. New York City, USA

Using the [USGS Historical Topographic Map Explorer](https://livingatlas.arcgis.com/topoexplorer/), I explored historical topographic map editions covering New York City.

I selected maps from different years and created a timelapse animation to visualize changes in the mapped landscape over time.

**Workflow:**
- Explored historical USGS topographic map editions.
- Selected maps representing different time periods.
- Created a timelapse animation to illustrate landscape changes.

## 📍 2. Nukus, Uzbekistan

For the second part, I worked with a historical Soviet-era topographic map of Nukus, Uzbekistan.

The map was published in **1985** and represents the geographical situation as of **1979**, at a scale of **1:100,000**.

Using **ArcGIS Pro**, I georeferenced the historical map and focused on the urban area of Nukus. I then compared the historical map with a modern OpenStreetMap (OSM) basemap to explore changes in the city's urban landscape.

**Workflow:**
- Used a scanned Soviet-era topographic map.
- Georeferenced the historical map in ArcGIS Pro.
- Focused on the urban area of Nukus.
- Compared historical cartographic information with a modern OpenStreetMap basemap.

